Wheel-Less Magnetic Road Vehicles: Full Six-Degree-of-Freedom Magnetic Mobility, Gap-Constrained Viability, and Controlled-Landing Fault Tolerance

Ouadi Maakoul · Claude · ChatGPT · DeepSeek

Third Draft — Corrected Architecture, Overhead-Rail EMS, and Viability-with-Target

---

Abstract

This paper extends the hybrid magnetic suspension architecture of Paper A to the wheel-less limit, with a corrected architectural foundation. Where Paper A treats the magnetic load fraction \lambda(t) \in [0,1) as a control variable for continuous load transfer between magnetic suspension and pneumatic wheels, the present work removes the wheels entirely and treats the magnetic suspension as the sole vertical support mechanism. The transition is formalized through a contact variable c(t) \in \{0,1\} such that Paper A corresponds to c=1 and the wheel-less architecture to c=0.

A critical architectural correction is made: attractive electromagnetic suspension (EMS) can only lift against an overhead ferromagnetic rail. The vehicle is therefore a rail-suspended, guideway-constrained road vehicle, not a vehicle on an ordinary road. The infrastructure assumption—a continuous overhead rail along the route—is explicit throughout.

We derive the vertical–pitch dynamics with corrected gap kinematics for attractive EMS, prove that attractive magnetic suspension is intrinsically unstable without mechanical contact, and formulate the full six-degree-of-freedom wrench allocation problem \mathbf{W} = B(q,v)\mathbf{F} with positive-span (force-closure) constraints. Six unilateral actuators are insufficient for full 6-DOF control; at least seven are required.

The safety envelope is reformulated from wheel-load positivity to gap confinement g_{\min} \leq g_i \leq g_{\max}. The fault-tolerant requirement becomes a viability-with-target problem: from the operating envelope, every admissible fault must have a trajectory into the landing target set \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing} that respects impact and deployment-time bounds. The admissibility condition is stated as a capture-basin inclusion, not a union.

A controlled-landing mode using deployable skids is introduced as the architectural fallback. Technical specifications are provided for a 500 kg reference vehicle, and the falsification criteria for a 10 m demonstrator are defined.

