1. Introduction

1.1 From Hybrid to Wheel-Less: Architectural Correction

Paper A established the mathematical foundations for a hybrid magnetic suspension in which pneumatic wheels are preserved and a continuously variable magnetic load fraction \lambda(t) \in [0,1) governs the vertical support distribution. The central constraint was wheel-load positivity:

F_{W,f} \geq F_{\min}, \qquad F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min}

and the fault-tolerant requirement was a controlled transition \lambda \to 0 with these constraints preserved throughout.

The present paper removes the wheels. The magnetic load fraction saturates to \lambda = 1, and the vertical equilibrium becomes

F_M = mg

in the ideal case.

However, a critical architectural correction is required. Attractive EMS cannot lift against a road below the vehicle—the road is below, so an attractive force toward it pulls the vehicle down, not up. Attractive lift requires an overhead ferromagnetic rail, as in the Transrapid system. The German Transrapid technology uses EMS with a nominal levitation gap of about 10 mm and achieves operating speeds above 430 km/h.

Therefore, the architecture considered here is a rail-suspended, guideway-constrained road vehicle: the vehicle is suspended from an overhead rail by attractive EMS, with the rail providing both lift and lateral guidance. The infrastructure assumption—a continuous overhead rail along the route—is explicit and non-negotiable.

The parameter \lambda ceases to be a transfer fraction and becomes an indicator of full magnetic support. The wheel-load constraint disappears. What replaces it is a gap constraint:

g_{\min} \leq g(t) \leq g_{\max}

where g(t) is the vehicle-to-rail air gap.

1.2 Problem Statement

The problem investigated here is not "the same vehicle without wheels." It is a different architectural class: a rail-suspended magnetic road vehicle in which the overhead rail and the vehicle together form a suspension system. The six degrees of freedom of the vehicle must be controlled magnetically, without permanent mechanical contact, while preserving a verifiable safety envelope under disturbances and faults.

The central question is:

\boxed{
\text{Can a rail-suspended road vehicle have all six degrees of freedom}
}

\boxed{
\text{controlled magnetically, without permanent mechanical contact,}
}

\boxed{
\text{while maintaining a verifiable safety envelope?}
}

1.3 Contributions

The contributions of this paper are sevenfold. First, it formalizes the contact variable c(t) \in \{0,1\} and shows that Paper A is the c=1 limit of the present architecture. Second, it corrects the architectural foundation: attractive EMS requires an overhead rail, making the vehicle rail-suspended and guideway-constrained. Third, it derives the vertical–pitch dynamics with corrected gap kinematics for attractive EMS and proves that attractive magnetic suspension is intrinsically unstable without mechanical contact. Fourth, it formulates the full six-degree-of-freedom wrench allocation \mathbf{W} = B(q,v)\mathbf{F} with positive-span (force-closure) constraints, showing that at least seven unilateral actuators are required. Fifth, it reformulates the safety envelope from wheel-load positivity to gap confinement and corrects the control barrier function formulation to a high-order CBF. Sixth, it reformulates the fault-tolerant requirement as a viability-with-target (capture basin) problem, correcting the vacuous union condition of the second draft. Seventh, it introduces the controlled-landing mode with deployable skids and states the admissibility condition as a capture-basin inclusion.

