3. Vertical–Pitch Dynamics and Intrinsic Instability

3.1 Magnetic Mechanism: Attractive EMS with Overhead Rail

The magnetic mechanism adopted in this paper is attractive electromagnetic suspension (EMS). The electromagnet is mounted on the vehicle and attracts toward an overhead ferromagnetic rail above the vehicle. The gap decreases when the vehicle moves toward the rail.

The attractive force is inversely proportional to the square of the gap:

F_M = K\left(\frac{I}{g}\right)^2

EMS systems are inherently unstable in the vertical direction without closed-loop control: any increase in gap reduces the attractive force, accelerating the gap further, so active correction must act faster than the disturbance dynamics. This is the intrinsic instability that Theorem 1 formalizes.

Infrastructure assumption: A continuous overhead ferromagnetic rail runs along the entire route. The rail provides both lift and lateral guidance. The vehicle is a rail-suspended, guideway-constrained road vehicle.

3.2 State Definition

The vertical–pitch state is

x_v = [z, \theta, \dot z, \dot\theta]^T

where z is the heave displacement (positive upward, toward the rail) and \theta is the pitch angle.

3.3 Corrected Gap Kinematics

For attractive EMS with an overhead rail, the gap decreases when the vehicle moves upward. With z measured upward and the rail fixed:

g_f = g_{f0} - z - x_{Mf}\theta

g_r = g_{r0} - z - x_{Mr}\theta

where g_{f0}, g_{r0} are the nominal gaps and x_{Mf} < 0 < x_{Mr} are the actuator coordinates.

For a true 6-DOF model, the gap kinematics should also include roll \phi and lateral offset y:

g_i = g_{i0} - z - x_i\theta - y_i\phi - y\sin\phi

The reduced vertical–pitch model retains the essential instability structure.

3.4 Magnetic Forces

The magnetic forces are functions of the gaps, currents, velocity, and shunt state:

F_{Mf} = F_{Mf}(g_f, I_f, v, s)

F_{Mr} = F_{Mr}(g_r, I_r, v, s)

For EMS, the force is attractive:

F_{Mi} = K_i\left(\frac{I_i}{g_i}\right)^2

with

\frac{\partial F_{Mi}}{\partial g_i} = -2K_i\frac{I_i^2}{g_i^3} < 0

3.5 Vertical–Pitch Dynamics

The vertical and pitch dynamics are

m\ddot z = F_{Mf}(g_f) + F_{Mr}(g_r) - mg + d_z

I_y\ddot\theta = x_{Mf}F_{Mf}(g_f) + x_{Mr}F_{Mr}(g_r) + M_{d,\theta}

3.6 Gap–Force Jacobian

Define the gap–force Jacobian

J = \begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{\partial F_{Mf}}{\partial g_f} + \dfrac{\partial F_{Mr}}{\partial g_r} & x_{Mf}\dfrac{\partial F_{Mf}}{\partial g_f} + x_{Mr}\dfrac{\partial F_{Mr}}{\partial g_r} \\[3mm]
x_{Mf}\dfrac{\partial F_{Mf}}{\partial g_f} + x_{Mr}\dfrac{\partial F_{Mr}}{\partial g_r} & x_{Mf}^2\dfrac{\partial F_{Mf}}{\partial g_f} + x_{Mr}^2\dfrac{\partial F_{Mr}}{\partial g_r}
\end{bmatrix}

3.7 Theorem: Intrinsic Instability (Corrected)

Theorem 1 (Intrinsic instability of attractive suspension). For an attractive magnetic suspension with

\frac{\partial F_{Mi}}{\partial g_i} < 0, \qquad i \in \{f, r\}

and gap kinematics g_i = g_{i0} - z - x_i\theta, the linearized stiffness matrix is -J. Since J is negative definite, -J is positive definite, and the linearized vertical–pitch dynamics has two unstable modes: a heave mode and a pitch mode.

Proof. Let

a = \frac{\partial F_{Mf}}{\partial g_f} < 0, \qquad b = \frac{\partial F_{Mr}}{\partial g_r} < 0

(using a and b to avoid confusion with velocity v).

Then

J = \begin{bmatrix} a+b & x_{Mf}a + x_{Mr}b \\ x_{Mf}a + x_{Mr}b & x_{Mf}^2 a + x_{Mr}^2 b \end{bmatrix}

Sylvester's criterion. For J to be negative definite, we need the leading principal minors to alternate in sign.

First minor:

\Delta_1 = a + b < 0

since a < 0 and b < 0.

Second minor (determinant):

\det(J) = (a+b)(x_{Mf}^2 a + x_{Mr}^2 b) - (x_{Mf}a + x_{Mr}b)^2

Expanding:

\det(J) = a^2 x_{Mf}^2 + ab x_{Mr}^2 + ab x_{Mf}^2 + b^2 x_{Mr}^2 - a^2 x_{Mf}^2 - 2ab x_{Mf}x_{Mr} - b^2 x_{Mr}^2

= ab(x_{Mr}^2 + x_{Mf}^2 - 2x_{Mf}x_{Mr})

= ab(x_{Mr} - x_{Mf})^2

Since a < 0 and b < 0, we have ab > 0. Since x_{Mf} \neq x_{Mr}, we have (x_{Mr} - x_{Mf})^2 > 0. Therefore

\det(J) > 0

With \Delta_1 < 0 and \det(J) > 0, Sylvester's criterion confirms that J is negative definite.

Now, the gap kinematics g_i = g_{i0} - z - x_i\theta imply that the linearized magnetic force perturbation is

\delta F_{Mi} = \frac{\partial F_{Mi}}{\partial g_i}\delta g_i = -\frac{\partial F_{Mi}}{\partial g_i}\delta z - x_i\frac{\partial F_{Mi}}{\partial g_i}\delta\theta

The effective stiffness matrix is

K_{\rm eff} = -J

Since J is negative definite, -J is positive definite. The linearized dynamics is

\begin{bmatrix} m & 0 \\ 0 & I_y \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} \ddot z \\ \ddot\theta \end{bmatrix}
=
(-J)
\begin{bmatrix} z \\ \theta \end{bmatrix}

The eigenvalues of M^{-1}(-J) are all positive, so the characteristic equation s^2 = \lambda > 0 has roots s = \pm\sqrt{\lambda}, giving two real positive eigenvalues. Both the heave and pitch modes are unstable. ∎

3.8 Corollary: Unstable Eigenmodes

Corollary 1. The linearized vertical–pitch dynamics has two unstable modes: a heave mode and a pitch mode.

Proof. The linearized dynamics is

\begin{bmatrix} \ddot z \\ \ddot\theta \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} m & 0 \\ 0 & I_y \end{bmatrix}^{-1} (-J) \begin{bmatrix} z \\ \theta \end{bmatrix}

Since -J is positive definite, both eigenvalues of M^{-1}(-J) are positive. Hence both modes are unstable. ∎

3.9 Remark on EDS and Hybrid Systems

For a repulsive electrodynamic suspension (EDS), the force–gap derivative can be positive in certain operating regimes, which can provide passive stability. EDS is passively stable at speed: the repulsive force increases with gap reduction, acting as a magnetic spring. However, EDS provides no levitation force at rest or very low speeds, so EDS vehicles require retractable wheels for station operations. The theorem's premise (\partial F/\partial g < 0) applies to attractive EMS. For EDS, the analysis changes, and the v_{\min} = 0 claim does not hold.

The reference vehicle in this paper is EMS-based and therefore requires active control at all speeds, including standstill.

