4. Full Six-Degree-of-Freedom Wrench Allocation

4.1 Wrench Definition

The full wrench applied to the vehicle is

\mathbf{W} = [F_x, F_y, F_z, M_x, M_y, M_z]^T

The magnetic wrench is the sum of contributions from n magnetic actuators:

\mathbf{W}_{\rm mag} = \sum_{i=1}^n B_i(q) F_i

where F_i is the force from actuator i and B_i(q) \in \mathbb{R}^6 is the actuator influence vector.

4.2 Matrix Form

Define

B(q, v) = [B_1(q), B_2(q), \ldots, B_n(q)] \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times n}

\mathbf{F} = [F_1, F_2, \ldots, F_n]^T \in \mathbb{R}^n

Then

\boxed{
\mathbf{W}_{\rm mag} = B(q, v)\mathbf{F}
}

This is the wrench–current vector formulation used in maglev planar motors, where a position-dependent transformation matrix maps the desired wrench to the corresponding coil currents. The decoupling matrix \mathbf{K}^+ must be a right inverse of the wrench matrix \mathbf{K} to fulfill \mathbf{K}\mathbf{K}^+ = \mathbf{I}. For a 6-DOF system, the rank of the matrix should be equal to six for all positions and orientations of the translator.

4.3 Positive-Span (Force-Closure) Condition

For attractive EMS, the force is unilateral:

F_i \geq 0

With F_i \geq 0, rank(B) = 6 is not sufficient for full 6-DOF control. The correct condition is positive span (force closure): the set of actuator wrenches must positively span \mathbb{R}^6.

A positive span of \mathbb{R}^n is guaranteed by a set of vectors if and only if the convex hull of the vectors contains the origin. Furthermore, n+1 vectors are both necessary and sufficient to positively span \mathbb{R}^n. Therefore, seven points are necessary and sufficient to ensure frictionless form closure.

For 6-DOF control, this means:

\boxed{
n \geq 7
}

Six unilateral actuators cannot positively span \mathbb{R}^6. At least seven are required. A positive span cannot be accomplished if one or more of the seven wrenches vanishes, that is, if the corresponding \lambda_i = 0, and negative \lambda_i are not permissible since they correspond to pulling forces.

4.4 Actuator Polytope

Each actuator force is bounded:

0 \leq F_i \leq F_{i,\max}

The admissible force set is the polytope

\mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{F} : 0 \leq F_i \leq F_{i,\max}, \; i = 1, \ldots, n\}

4.5 Gap Constraint

The gap at actuator i is a function of the vehicle state:

g_i = g_i(q)

The gap constraint is

g_{\min} \leq g_i(q) \leq g_{\max}, \qquad i = 1, \ldots, n

4.6 Allocation Problem

The wrench allocation problem is

\min_{\mathbf{F}} \|\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{F}_{\rm nom}\|^2

subject to

B(q, v)\mathbf{F} = \mathbf{W}_d

\mathbf{F} \in \mathcal{F}

g_{\min} \leq g_i(q) \leq g_{\max}

The solution can be obtained via the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse:

\mathbf{K}^+ = \mathbf{K}^{\mathrm{T}}(\mathbf{K}\mathbf{K}^{\mathrm{T}})^{-1}

for the unconstrained case, with projection onto \mathcal{F} for the constrained case.

4.7 Feasibility Condition

The allocation is feasible if and only if

\boxed{
B(q, v)\mathcal{F} \cap \{\mathbf{W}_d\} \neq \varnothing
}

and

\boxed{
g_{\min} \leq g_i(q) \leq g_{\max}, \qquad i = 1, \ldots, n
}

This is the correct feasibility condition. The positive-span condition n \geq 7 is a necessary prerequisite.

4.8 Actuator Configuration

For n = 7 unilateral actuators, a symmetric arrangement is proposed:

Actuator Position (x, y, z) Primary Function
1 (-0.7, +0.3, 0) Front-left vertical
2 (-0.7, -0.3, 0) Front-right vertical
3 (+0.7, +0.3, 0) Rear-left vertical
4 (+0.7, -0.3, 0) Rear-right vertical
5 (0, +0.5, 0) Lateral/yaw
6 (0, -0.5, 0) Lateral/yaw
7 (0, 0, 0) Longitudinal/redundant

This arrangement provides positive span for the 6-DOF wrench allocation with unilateral forces.

Longitudinal force generation. A normal-force EMS module produces essentially no F_x. Longitudinal force must be generated by a separate mechanism: either a linear induction motor (as in Transrapid) or a dedicated longitudinal actuator. The \pm 1472 N longitudinal requirement is provided by the linear motor, not by the normal-force EMS modules.

