5. Safety Envelope as Gap Confinement

5.1 From Wheel-Load Positivity to Gap Confinement

In Paper A, the safety constraint was

F_{W,f} \geq F_{\min}, \qquad F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min}

In Paper B, the wheels are absent, so this constraint is replaced by

g_{\min} \leq g_i \leq g_{\max}, \qquad i = 1, \ldots, n

The gap constraint ensures:

· The vehicle does not touch the rail (g_i > g_{\min})
· The vehicle does not lose magnetic force (g_i < g_{\max})
· The vehicle does not enter an unstable region of the magnetic suspension

The literature confirms that gap confinement is the primary safety constraint for maglev systems. A barrier function has been introduced to address the physical contact problem, strictly limiting the levitation gap within an allowable range.

5.2 Safe Set

The safe set is

\mathcal{K} = \left\{ x : \begin{array}{l}
g_{\min} \leq g_i(q) \leq g_{\max}, \quad i = 1, \ldots, n \\
|\phi| \leq \phi_{\max}, \quad |\theta| \leq \theta_{\max} \\
|r| \leq r_{\max} \\
v_{\min} \leq v \leq v_{\max} \\
0 \leq F_i \leq F_{i,\max} \\
P \leq P_{\max}
\end{array} \right\}

For EMS, v_{\min} = 0 is admissible.

5.3 Gap Rate Constraint

|\dot g_i| \leq \dot g_{\max}

5.4 Gap Acceleration Constraint

|\ddot g_i| \leq \ddot g_{\max}

5.5 High-Order Control Barrier Function

The gap g has relative degree 2 with respect to the force input: the force affects acceleration, which integrates to velocity, which integrates to gap. Therefore, the standard CBF condition

\dot h + \alpha(h) \geq 0

cannot be enforced by the input directly. The correct formulation uses a high-order control barrier function (HOCBF) or exponential control barrier function (ECBF).

For the lower gap constraint, define

h(x) = g(x) - g_{\min}

The HOCBF condition is

\ddot h + K_1 \dot h + K_0 h \geq 0

where K_1, K_0 > 0 are chosen so that the polynomial s^2 + K_1 s + K_0 is Hurwitz. This ensures that h remains non-negative.

For the upper gap constraint, define

\bar h(x) = g_{\max} - g(x)

The HOCBF condition is

\ddot{\bar h} + K_1 \dot{\bar h} + K_0 \bar h \geq 0

The use of high-order CBFs for relative-degree-two constraints has been validated for magnetic levitation systems. Exponential control barrier functions (ECBFs) have been successfully applied to the maglev system for safety constraint problems with high relative degree.

