6. Viability Without a Passive Attractor: Viability-with-Target

6.1 The Structural Difference from Paper A

In Paper A, the state (\lambda = 0, u = 0) was always in the safe set because the wheels supported the vehicle. The viability kernel was non-empty by construction.

In Paper B, there is no passive safe state. The magnetic suspension must be actively controlled at all times. If control is lost, the vehicle falls.

6.2 Viability Kernel

The robust viability kernel is

\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust} = \operatorname{Viab}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{U}_{\rm nom}, \mathcal{U}_{\rm fault})

This is the set of states from which there exists a control trajectory that keeps the system in \mathcal{K} for all time, under nominal and fault conditions.

The viability kernel is the largest closed subset D satisfying C \subset D \subset K and

F(x) \cap T_D(x) \neq \emptyset

where F is the set-valued dynamics and T_D(x) is the contingent cone to D at x.

6.3 Existence Condition

The architecture is admissible if

\boxed{
\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust} \neq \varnothing
}

Unlike Paper A, this condition is not automatically satisfied. It must be verified.

6.4 Viability-with-Target (Capture Basin)

The correct formulation of the fault-tolerant requirement is viability-with-target, not a simple union.

Define the landing target set \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing} as the set of states from which the vehicle can safely complete a controlled landing:

\mathcal{C}_{\rm landing} = \left\{ x : \begin{array}{l}
g_i \geq g_{\rm contact} \\
\dot z \geq -\dot z_{\rm impact} \\
\text{skid deployment feasible} \\
\text{impact energy absorbable}
\end{array} \right\}

The capture basin (viable-capture basin) is the set of initial states x_0 \in \mathcal{K} from which at least one evolution starting at x_0 is viable in \mathcal{K} until it reaches \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing} in finite time:

\mathcal{K}_{\rm capture} = \operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})

The architecture is admissible if

\boxed{
\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\rm capture}
}

This means that from every state in the robust magnetic viability kernel, there exists a fault-response trajectory that reaches the landing target set within the impact and deployment-time bounds. This is a stronger and non-vacuous condition than the union condition of the second draft.

6.5 Why the Union Condition Was Vacuous

The second draft stated

\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \cup \mathcal{K}_{\rm landing} \neq \varnothing

This is vacuous because \mathcal{K}_{\rm landing} is non-empty trivially (the vehicle parked on skids is a valid landing state). The union can never be empty. The meaningful test is that every admissible fault from the operating envelope has a trajectory into \mathcal{K}_{\rm landing}.

6.6 Sufficient Condition for Capture

A sufficient condition for \mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\rm capture} is the existence of a control Lyapunov function V(x) and a control invariant set \Omega \subset \mathcal{K} such that

\dot V(x) \leq -\alpha V(x) + \beta \|d(t)\|

for all x \in \Omega, with \alpha > 0 and \beta bounded, and

\Omega \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\rm capture}

