7. Controlled Landing and Admissibility

7.1 The Fault Problem Without Wheels

In Paper A, the fault-tolerant requirement was

\text{fault} \Rightarrow \text{stable wheel-supported state}

In Paper B, there is no wheel-supported state. The fault-tolerant requirement becomes

\text{fault} \Rightarrow \text{controlled landing on skids}

7.2 Controlled Landing Mode

A controlled landing mode is defined by the existence of a trajectory from the fault state to a contact state:

x(t_f) \to x_{\rm contact}

with

\dot z(t_f) \geq -\dot z_{\rm impact}

and

F_{S,i}(t_f^+) \geq F_{\min}

where F_{S,i} is the skid load (not wheel load).

7.3 Landing Mechanisms: Deployable Skids

On failure of the magnetic suspension, the vehicle touches down on the rail configuration by means of emergency gliding elements like sliding shoes, skids, or the like. The touch-down shock of the emergency gliding elements must be limited to a force maximum as small as possible.

A spring-attenuator combination is used: the spring characteristic being linear while the attenuator has a square-law characteristic for the compressing and a linear characteristic for the yielding motion. The force maximum thus achieved is exactly half the size of the physically smallest possible force maximum for an emergency gliding element sprung without damping.

For the reference vehicle, each bogie is equipped with four landing skids, two mounted underneath and on each end of the bogie. The landing skids could be lowered such that the skid shoes come into contact with the guideway surface.

7.4 Landing Viability Kernel

The landing viability kernel is

\mathcal{K}_{\rm landing} = \operatorname{Viab}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{U}_{\rm landing})

where \mathcal{U}_{\rm landing} is the set of admissible landing controls. This must incorporate:

· Deployment time t_{\rm deploy}
· Stroke s_{\max}
· Damping coefficient c_{\rm damp}
· Stroke limit s_{\rm limit}
· Impact velocity bound \dot z_{\rm impact}

7.5 Admissibility Condition (Corrected)

The architecture is admissible if

\boxed{
\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})
}

This means that from every state in the robust magnetic viability kernel, there exists a fault-response trajectory that reaches the landing target set within the impact and deployment-time bounds.

7.6 Falsification Condition (Corrected)

The architecture is falsified if

\boxed{
\exists \, x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} : x_0 \notin \operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})
}

This means that there exists a state in the magnetic viability kernel from which no admissible fault-response trajectory reaches the landing target set. This is a non-vacuous falsification condition.

