11. Conclusion

This paper has extended the hybrid magnetic suspension architecture of Paper A to the wheel-less limit, with a corrected architectural foundation.

The key results are:

1. Architectural correction. Attractive EMS requires an overhead ferromagnetic rail. The vehicle is a rail-suspended, guideway-constrained road vehicle, not a vehicle on an ordinary road.
2. Intrinsic instability theorem. Attractive magnetic suspension is unstable without mechanical contact. The corrected gap kinematics g_i = g_{i0} - z - x_i\theta yields a stiffness matrix -J that is positive definite, giving two unstable modes.
3. Positive-span wrench allocation. Six unilateral actuators cannot positively span \mathbb{R}^6. At least seven are required for full 6-DOF control with unilateral forces.
4. Gap confinement with high-order CBF. The gap constraint has relative degree 2 with respect to force. A high-order CBF (or ECBF) is required, not the standard first-order CBF.
5. Viability-with-target. The fault-tolerant requirement is a capture-basin inclusion:

\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})

not a vacuous union condition.

6. Controlled landing on skids. Deployable skids with spring-attenuator combinations provide the architectural fallback for fault tolerance.

The core that remains is:

\boxed{
\text{6-DOF magnetic control} +
\text{positive-span allocation} +
\text{gap confinement} +
\text{capture-basin fault tolerance}
}

with the true test being

\boxed{
\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})
}

rather than simply "the vehicle could levitate." The next step is the numerical construction of the achievable wrench set, the gap confinement set, and the capture basin simultaneously. If this inclusion fails, the project terminates. If it holds, the 6-DOF controller and 10 m demonstrator are justified.

