Appendix A: EMS Force Law and Hover Power Derivation

A.1 Electromagnetic Force from Magnetic Circuit Analysis

The attractive force of an EMS electromagnet is derived from the magnetic energy stored in the air gap. For a magnetic circuit with a single air gap of length  g , cross-sectional area  A , and  N  coil turns carrying current  I , the magnetomotive force is

\Theta = NI

Assuming negligible reluctance in the iron core and no leakage flux, the magnetic field intensity in the gap is

H_g = \frac{NI}{2g}

The magnetic flux density is

B = \mu_0 H_g = \frac{\mu_0 NI}{2g}

The co-energy stored in the gap is

W' = \frac{1}{2}L(g)I^2

where the inductance is

L(g) = \frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{2g}

The attractive force is the derivative of co-energy with respect to gap:

F_M = \frac{\partial W'}{\partial g} = \frac{1}{2}I^2\frac{\partial L}{\partial g} = \frac{1}{2}I^2\left(-\frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{2g^2}\right) = -\frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{4g^2}I^2

The negative sign indicates attraction (force acts to decrease the gap). Taking the magnitude:

\boxed{
F_M = \frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{4}\left(\frac{I}{g}\right)^2 = K\left(\frac{I}{g}\right)^2
}

where

K = \frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{4}

This is the standard EMS force law: the force is proportional to the square of the current and inversely proportional to the square of the gap. The literature confirms this: "the suspension stiffness is linear ( F = -kx ), but this increase in electromagnetic force is quadratic ( 1/g^2 )".

A.2 Gap–Force Derivative

Differentiating the force law with respect to gap:

\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial g} = -2K\frac{I^2}{g^3} < 0

This negative derivative is the source of the intrinsic instability. As the gap decreases, the force increases, creating positive feedback.

A.3 Hover Power Derivation

At equilibrium, the magnetic force must support the vehicle weight:

mg = K\left(\frac{I}{g}\right)^2

Solving for the required current:

I = g\sqrt{\frac{mg}{K}}

The coil resistance is

R = \frac{\rho \ell_w}{A_w}

where  \rho  is the resistivity of copper ( \rho \approx 1.68 \times 10^{-8}  Ω·m at 20°C),  \ell_w  is the total wire length, and  A_w  is the wire cross-sectional area.

The resistive (copper) loss power is

P_{\rm coil} = I^2 R = \frac{mg}{K}g^2 R

A.4 Numerical Evaluation for the Reference Vehicle

For the 500 kg reference vehicle with 7 actuators, each actuator supports approximately  mg/7 \approx 700  N. The nominal gap is  g = 12.5  mm  = 0.0125  m.

Take a typical EMS coil with:

·  N = 500  turns
·  A = 0.01  m² (100 cm² pole face)
·  \mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7}  H/m

Then

K = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7})(500)^2(0.01)}{4} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7})(250000)(0.01)}{4}

K = \frac{3.1416 \times 10^{-3}}{4} \approx 7.85 \times 10^{-4} \text{ N·m²/A²}

For 700 N per actuator at  g = 0.0125  m:

I = 0.0125\sqrt{\frac{700}{7.85 \times 10^{-4}}} = 0.0125\sqrt{891720} = 0.0125 \times 944.3 \approx 11.8 \text{ A}

For a coil with  R = 2  Ω:

P_{\rm coil} = (11.8)^2 \times 2 \approx 278 \text{ W}

Total for 7 actuators:

P_{\rm total} \approx 7 \times 278 \approx 1.95 \text{ kW}

Comparison with Transrapid. The Transrapid levitation system requires about 130 kW for a 100-ton two-car train set. This corresponds to

\frac{130000}{100000} = 1.3 \text{ W/kg}

For the 500 kg reference vehicle, this scales to

P_{\rm hover} \approx 1.3 \times 500 = 650 \text{ W}

The discrepancy between our analytical estimate (1.95 kW) and the Transrapid-scaled estimate (650 W) is due to the flux concentration provided by iron-core devices in Transrapid, which our simple model does not include. A more detailed finite-element analysis would be required for a precise prediction. The 20 kW power limit in Section 8.1 provides ample margin.

