Appendix B: Positive Span and Force Closure — Proof and Construction

B.1 Definition of Positive Span

A set of vectors  \{v_1, \ldots, v_n\}  in  \mathbb{R}^n  positively spans  \mathbb{R}^n  if every vector  w \in \mathbb{R}^n  can be expressed as

w = \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i v_i, \qquad \alpha_i \geq 0

This is equivalent to the condition that the convex hull of the vectors contains the origin.

B.2 Fundamental Theorem

Theorem B.1. In  \mathbb{R}^n ,  n+1  vectors are both necessary and sufficient to positively span  \mathbb{R}^n . Therefore, seven points are necessary and sufficient to ensure frictionless form closure in 6-dimensional wrench space.

Proof (Necessity). Suppose  n  vectors positively span  \mathbb{R}^n . Then there exist  \alpha_i \geq 0 , not all zero, such that

\sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i v_i = 0

This is a positive linear dependence, which means the vectors lie in a closed half-space. They cannot positively span  \mathbb{R}^n . Therefore, at least  n+1  vectors are required.

Proof (Sufficiency). Let  \{v_1, \ldots, v_{n+1}\}  be a set of  n+1  vectors in general position such that the origin is in the interior of their convex hull. Any  w \in \mathbb{R}^n  can be written as a positive combination of these vectors. The convex hull of  n+1  points in general position in  \mathbb{R}^n  is an  n -simplex. If the origin is in its interior, then every point in  \mathbb{R}^n  can be expressed as a positive combination of the vertices. ∎

B.3 Application to the 7-Actuator Configuration

The 7 actuators at positions given in Table 8.5 produce influence vectors  B_i \in \mathbb{R}^6 . For each actuator, the influence vector is constructed from the force direction and the moment arm:

B_i = \begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{f}_i \\
\mathbf{r}_i \times \mathbf{f}_i
\end{bmatrix}

where  \mathbf{f}_i  is the unit force direction and  \mathbf{r}_i  is the position vector of actuator  i  relative to the center of mass.

For the configuration with actuators at  (-0.7, \pm 0.3, 0) ,  (+0.7, \pm 0.3, 0) ,  (0, \pm 0.5, 0) , and  (0, 0, 0) , the wrench matrix  B \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times 7}  has full row rank 6. The nullspace of  B  is one-dimensional. For positive span, we require that the nullspace vector  v  (with  Bv = 0 ) has purely positive coefficients. When the nullspace vector  v  has purely positive coefficients, the nullspace vector may be scaled by its  L_1  norm to yield the vector  u = v/\|v\|_1 , which is also an element of the nullspace of  B . The components of  u  are the  \lambda_v , from which it can be checked that  \sum \lambda_v = 1 , implying the origin is expressed as a convex combination of the  \mathbf{w}_i  and is therefore contained in the convex hull of the wrenches.

A positive span cannot be accomplished if one or more of the seven wrenches vanishes, that is, if the corresponding  \lambda_i = 0 , and negative  \lambda_i  are not permissible since they correspond to pulling forces applied along the outward surface normal.

B.4 Verification Procedure

To verify positive span numerically:

1. Compute the  6 \times 7  wrench matrix  B .
2. Compute the nullspace vector  v  satisfying  Bv = 0 .
3. Normalize:  u = v/\|v\|_1 .
4. Check that all components of  u  are strictly positive.
5. If yes, the origin is in the interior of the convex hull, and the configuration achieves positive span.

For the proposed 7-actuator geometry, this verification should be performed numerically for the nominal pose and at the extremes of the operating envelope.

