Appendix C: High-Order Control Barrier Function Construction

C.1 Relative Degree and the Need for HOCBF

The gap  g(x)  has relative degree 2 with respect to the force input  F :

g \xrightarrow{\text{integrate}} \dot g \xrightarrow{\text{integrate}} \ddot g \propto F

The standard CBF condition  \dot h + \alpha(h) \geq 0  involves only the first derivative of  h , which depends on velocity, not force. Therefore, it cannot be enforced by the input directly.

C.2 Exponential Control Barrier Function (ECBF)

For a constraint function  h(x)  with relative degree  r , the ECBF condition is

h^{(r)} + K_{r-1}h^{(r-1)} + \cdots + K_1\dot h + K_0 h \geq 0

where the coefficients  K_i > 0  are chosen so that the polynomial

s^r + K_{r-1}s^{r-1} + \cdots + K_1 s + K_0

is Hurwitz (all roots have negative real part).

For the gap constraint with  r = 2 :

\ddot h + K_1 \dot h + K_0 h \geq 0

The literature confirms that "multiple safety constraints with relative-degree greater than one were applied. One way to deal with this is to use Exponential Control Barrier Functions (ECBFs)". The ECBF approach has been successfully applied to MIMO magnetic levitation systems.

C.3 Construction for the Lower Gap Constraint

Define

h(x) = g(x) - g_{\min}

The first derivative is

\dot h = \dot g = -\dot z - x_i\dot\theta

The second derivative is

\ddot h = \ddot g = -\ddot z - x_i\ddot\theta

From the vertical–pitch dynamics:

m\ddot z = F_{Mf} + F_{Mr} - mg + d_z

I_y\ddot\theta = x_{Mf}F_{Mf} + x_{Mr}F_{Mr} + M_{d,\theta}

Substituting:

\ddot h = -\frac{F_{Mf} + F_{Mr} - mg + d_z}{m} - x_i\frac{x_{Mf}F_{Mf} + x_{Mr}F_{Mr} + M_{d,\theta}}{I_y}

The ECBF condition is

\ddot h + K_1 \dot h + K_0 h \geq 0

This is linear in the control forces  F_{Mf}  and  F_{Mr} , so it can be enforced via quadratic programming (QP):

\min_{\mathbf{F}} \|\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{F}_{\rm nom}\|^2

subject to

\ddot h + K_1 \dot h + K_0 h \geq 0

C.4 Construction for the Upper Gap Constraint

Define

\bar h(x) = g_{\max} - g(x)

The ECBF condition is

\ddot{\bar h} + K_1 \dot{\bar h} + K_0 \bar h \geq 0

which expands to

-\ddot g + K_1(-\dot g) + K_0(g_{\max} - g) \geq 0

C.5 Choice of Gains

The gains  K_1  and  K_0  are chosen so that the polynomial  s^2 + K_1 s + K_0  is Hurwitz. A common choice is

K_1 = 2\zeta\omega_n, \qquad K_0 = \omega_n^2

where  \zeta > 0  is the damping ratio and  \omega_n > 0  is the natural frequency. For the reference vehicle with gap dynamics bandwidth of approximately 10 Hz:

\omega_n = 2\pi \times 10 \approx 62.8 \text{ rad/s}

K_1 = 2 \times 0.7 \times 62.8 \approx 88

K_0 = 62.8^2 \approx 3944

