Appendix D: Viability Kernel and Capture Basin — Formal Definitions

D.1 Viability Kernel

Consider the control system

\dot x \in F(x, u), \qquad u \in \mathcal{U}

where  F  is a set-valued map and  \mathcal{U}  is the set of admissible controls.

The viability kernel of a set  K \subset \mathbb{R}^n  is the largest closed subset  D \subset K  such that for every  x_0 \in D , there exists a control trajectory  x(t)  with  x(0) = x_0  that remains in  K  for all  t \geq 0 .

Formally:

\operatorname{Viab}(K) = \{x_0 \in K : \exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}, \; \forall t \geq 0, \; x(t) \in K\}

The viability kernel is the largest closed subset  D  satisfying  C \subset D \subset K  and

F(x) \cap T_D(x) \neq \emptyset

where  T_D(x)  is the contingent cone to  D  at  x , defined as the set of directions  v  such that

\liminf_{h \to 0^+} \frac{d_D(x + hv)}{h} = 0

where  d_D  is the distance to  D .

D.2 Capture Basin (Viability-with-Target)

Given a target set  \mathcal{C} \subset K , the capture basin (or viable-capture basin) is the set of initial states  x_0 \in K  from which at least one evolution starting at  x_0  is viable in  K  until it reaches  \mathcal{C}  in finite time:

\operatorname{Capt}(K, \mathcal{C}) = \left\{x_0 \in K : \exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}, \; \exists T \geq 0, \; \begin{aligned}
&x(t) \in K \quad \forall t \in [0, T] \\
&x(T) \in \mathcal{C}
\end{aligned}\right\}

The capture basin is the unique closed set  D  satisfying  \mathcal{C} \subset D \subset K  and

\forall x \in D \setminus \mathcal{C}, \quad F(x) \cap T_D(x) \neq \emptyset

This characterization is established in Aubin’s theorem: “viability kernels and capture basins of a target viable in a constrained subset as a unique closed subset between the target and the constrained subset satisfying tangential conditions or, by duality, normal conditions”. The theory is based on a method devised by Hélène Frankowska for characterizing the value function of an optimal control problem as generalized (contingent or viscosity) solutions to Hamilton-Jacobi equations.

D.3 Application to the Fault-Tolerant Problem

For the wheel-less EMS vehicle:

·  K  is the safe set defined in Section 5.2.
·  \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing}  is the landing target set defined in Section 6.4.
·  \mathcal{U}_{\rm nom}  is the nominal control set.
·  \mathcal{U}_{\rm fault}  is the fault control set.

The robust magnetic viability kernel is

\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} = \operatorname{Viab}(K, \mathcal{U}_{\rm nom}, \mathcal{U}_{\rm fault})

The capture basin for landing is

\mathcal{K}_{\rm capture} = \operatorname{Capt}(K, \mathcal{C}_{\rm landing})

The architecture is admissible if

\boxed{
\mathcal{K}_{\rm robust}^{\rm mag} \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\rm capture}
}

D.4 Numerical Computation

The viability kernel and capture basin can be computed numerically using:

1. Grid-based methods: Discretize the state space and compute the viability kernel via fixed-point iteration:

D_{k+1} = \{x \in D_k : F(x) \cap T_{D_k}(x) \neq \emptyset\}

2. Level-set methods: Represent the viability kernel as the zero sublevel set of a function  \phi(x)  and solve the Hamilton-Jacobi partial differential equation.
3. Sampling-based methods: Use random sampling and trajectory simulation to approximate the viability kernel.

For the reduced vertical–pitch model with 4 states, a grid-based method with  50 \times 50 \times 50 \times 50  grid points is feasible. For the full 12-state 6-DOF model, sampling-based methods are required.

