Appendix F: Landing Dynamics and Impact Model

F.1 Spring-Attenuator Model

The emergency landing system uses a spring-attenuator combination where the spring characteristic is linear and the attenuator has a square-law characteristic for the compressing motion and a linear characteristic for the yielding motion.

The force during compression is

F_{\rm land}(s, \dot s) = k_s s + c_{\rm sq}\dot s^2, \qquad \dot s > 0

where  s  is the stroke,  k_s  is the spring stiffness, and  c_{\rm sq}  is the square-law damping coefficient.

The force during extension is

F_{\rm land}(s, \dot s) = k_s s + c_{\rm lin}\dot s, \qquad \dot s < 0

where  c_{\rm lin}  is the linear damping coefficient.

The force maximum achieved with this combination is exactly half the size of the physically smallest possible force maximum for an emergency gliding element sprung without damping.

F.2 Impact Velocity Bound

The maximum impact velocity is determined by the stroke limit  s_{\max}  and the maximum allowable force  F_{\max} :

\frac{1}{2}m\dot z_{\rm impact}^2 \leq \int_0^{s_{\max}} F_{\rm land}(s) \, ds

For the linear spring alone:

\frac{1}{2}m\dot z_{\rm impact}^2 \leq \frac{1}{2}k_s s_{\max}^2

\dot z_{\rm impact} \leq s_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{k_s}{m}}

For the reference vehicle with  m = 500  kg,  s_{\max} = 0.1  m, and  k_s = 10^5  N/m:

\dot z_{\rm impact} \leq 0.1\sqrt{\frac{10^5}{500}} = 0.1 \times 14.14 \approx 1.41 \text{ m/s}

F.3 Landing Viability Kernel

The landing viability kernel is the set of states from which a controlled landing can be achieved:

\mathcal{K}_{\rm landing} = \left\{ x : \begin{array}{l}
g_i \geq g_{\rm contact} \\
\dot z \geq -\dot z_{\rm impact} \\
t_{\rm deploy} \leq t_{\rm available} \\
s \leq s_{\max}
\end{array} \right\}

where  t_{\rm available}  is the time available before the gap reaches  g_{\rm contact} .

F.4 Deployment Time Constraint

The skids must deploy before the gap reaches  g_{\rm contact} . The time available is

t_{\rm available} = \frac{g - g_{\rm contact}}{-\dot z} \quad \text{for } \dot z < 0

The deployment constraint is

t_{\rm deploy} \leq t_{\rm available}

For a typical electromechanical deployment system,  t_{\rm deploy} \approx 0.1  s. With  g - g_{\rm contact} = 0.0125  m and  \dot z = -0.5  m/s:

t_{\rm available} = \frac{0.0125}{0.5} = 0.025 \text{ s}

This is less than  t_{\rm deploy} = 0.1  s, so deployment would not complete in time. A faster deployment mechanism or a larger gap margin is required. This is a critical design constraint.

