Appendix G: Linear Motor Thrust for Longitudinal Force

G.1 Transrapid LSM Configuration

The German Transrapid system uses an iron-cored long-stator linear synchronous motor (LSM) for propulsion. The magnets excited with controlled direct-current (DC) power are used aboard the vehicle, and the three-phase windings on the guideway are enclosed by ferromagnetic lamination cores.

G.2 Thrust Equation

The thrust force of a long-stator LSM is

F_x = \frac{3}{2}\frac{\pi}{\tau_p}\lambda_{\rm PM}I_q

where  \tau_p  is the pole pitch,  \lambda_{\rm PM}  is the permanent magnet flux linkage, and  I_q  is the quadrature-axis current.

G.3 Numerical Values for the Reference Vehicle

For the Transrapid, the nominal current is  I_s = 1200  A and the electric loading is  A_s = 2mNI_s/(2p\tau_p) . For the reference vehicle with a required thrust of 1472 N:

F_x = 1472 \text{ N}

Assuming a pole pitch of  \tau_p = 0.2  m and a flux linkage of  \lambda_{\rm PM} = 0.5  Wb:

I_q = \frac{2F_x\tau_p}{3\pi\lambda_{\rm PM}} = \frac{2 \times 1472 \times 0.2}{3\pi \times 0.5} = \frac{588.8}{4.712} \approx 125 \text{ A}

This is well within the Transrapid’s nominal current of 1200 A.

G.4 Separation of Levitation and Propulsion

The EMS normal-force modules provide levitation and lateral guidance. The LSM provides longitudinal thrust. This separation is consistent with the Transrapid architecture, where the levitation and guidance functions are separate from the propulsion function.

